---
// Direction 4, "Paper": /v/2009 refined into an actual 2008-era cryptography
// paper. Letter-size sheets on a grey surround, Computer Modern (CMU Serif,
// subset to this page's glyphs and renamed "Sova Paper Serif" as the OFL's
// reserved-name clause asks of modified versions), justified and hyphenated,
// numbered sections, display equations, line-drawn figures and references.
// On wide screens a sticky table of contents sits in the left margin, outside
// the sheets; narrower screens get a "Contents" disclosure on the first sheet.
//
// The copy is paper v5 (docs/paper/sova-paper-v5.md), set as written; the
// section map, figure specs and the source of every claim are in
// docs/paper/v5-notes.md and site/README.md ("Where each claim comes from").
//
// Glyphs: the subset font covers printable ASCII plus · × Σ — ’ “ ” … → −
// (and no-break space). Every rendered character on this page, figure text
// included, must stay inside that set. There is no U+230A/B, so floor
// brackets are drawn with borders.
//
// Anchors: section ids are names, not numbers (#intro … #conclusion, #refs).
// The ids of the v4 paper (#s1 … #s10, #s3-1 … #s5-2, #s-zcash, #fig4) are
// kept as empty spans at the equivalent place, so old links still land;
// #fig1 … #fig3 are the v5 figures, which show the same ideas as v4's.
import Direction from '../layouts/Direction.astro';
import DirSwitch from './DirSwitch.astro';
import { EDGE_LINE, footerLinks, repo, sips, WORDMARK_PATHS, sitePages as allPages } from '../data/sova';

interface Props {
  /** The public homepage: indexable, no design-direction switcher. */
  home?: boolean;
}
const { home = false } = Astro.props;
const TITLE = 'Sova: An EVM Chain That Can See Zcash';
// Links out of the paper: home first, then every page but this one.
const sitePages = [{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, ...allPages.filter((p) => p.href !== '/paper')];
// Reference URL of a SIP's text on GitHub (data/sova.ts `sips[].file`).
// SIP-7 is not in that list yet, so its file is named here.
const sipUrl = (n: number) => repo.blob(`sips/${sips.find((s) => s.n === n)!.file}`);
const sip7Url = repo.blob('sips/sip-7-draft-zcash-events.md');

// Table of contents: every numbered heading on the page, in order.
const toc: { id: string; n: string; t: string }[] = [
  { id: 'intro', n: '1', t: 'Introduction' },
  { id: 'burns', n: '2', t: 'Burns' },
  { id: 'epochs', n: '3', t: 'Epochs' },
  { id: 'issuance', n: '4', t: 'Issuance' },
  { id: 'sealing', n: '5', t: 'Sealing' },
  { id: 'network', n: '6', t: 'Network' },
  { id: 'verification', n: '7', t: 'Verification' },
  { id: 'reading-zcash', n: '8', t: 'Reading Zcash' },
  { id: 'privacy', n: '9', t: 'Privacy' },
  { id: 'calculations', n: '10', t: 'Calculations' },
  { id: 'conclusion', n: '11', t: 'Conclusion' },
  { id: 'refs', n: '', t: 'References' },
];

// References [1]-[10], as in the paper.
const refs: { a: string; t: string; href: string; extra?: string }[] = [
  { a: 'D. Hopwood, S. Bowe, T. Hornby, N. Wilcox', t: 'Zcash Protocol Specification', href: 'https://zips.z.cash/protocol/protocol.pdf' },
  { a: 'Sova', t: 'SIP-1: The Burn Transaction Format', href: sipUrl(1) },
  { a: 'Sova', t: 'SIP-2: Epochs, Rewards, and Settlement', href: sipUrl(2) },
  { a: 'Sova', t: 'SIP-3: Emission Schedule', href: sipUrl(3) },
  { a: 'Sova', t: 'SIP-4: Zcash State Precompile', href: sipUrl(4) },
  { a: 'S. Nakamoto', t: 'Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System', extra: '2008', href: 'https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf' },
  { a: 'V. Buterin', t: 'Ethereum: A Next-Generation Smart Contract and Decentralized Application Platform', extra: '2014', href: 'https://ethereum.org/whitepaper' },
  { a: 'A. Dietrichs et al.', t: 'EIP-4895: Beacon chain push withdrawals as operations', href: 'https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-4895' },
  { a: 'Sova', t: 'SIP-6: Sealer Signatures', extra: '(accepted)', href: sipUrl(6) },
  { a: 'Sova', t: 'SIP-7: Zcash Pool State and Events', extra: '(accepted)', href: sip7Url },
];
---
<Direction
  title={TITLE}
  description="Sova is an EVM chain that can see Zcash: contracts check real ZEC payments on Zcash itself. SOVA, its gas, is mined by burning ZEC. Pre-release draft."
  theme="#d9d5cc"
  indexable={home}
>
  <link slot="head" rel="preload" href="/fonts/paper-serif-roman.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin />

  <header class="chrome">
    <a href={home ? "/" : "/v/paper"} class="mark" aria-label="Sova: home">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 387 70" width="60" height="11" aria-hidden="true">
        {WORDMARK_PATHS.map((d) => <path fill="currentColor" d={d} />)}
      </svg>
    </a>
    <span>Pre-release draft. No public network yet.</span>
  </header>

  <div class="layout">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <p class="toc-h">Contents</p>
      <ol class="toc-l">
        {toc.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`} data-toc={s.id}><span class="tn">{s.n}</span>{s.t}</a></li>)}
      </ol>
      <p class="toc-h">Elsewhere</p>
      <ul class="toc-x">
        {sitePages.map((p) => <li><a href={p.href}>{p.label}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>

  <main id="main" class="doc">
    <!-- Sheet 1 -->
    <article class="sheet" aria-label="Page 1">
      <header class="titleblock">
        <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
        <p class="author">Sova</p>
        <p class="meta">sova.io</p>
        <p class="meta">September 2026 (pre-release draft)</p>
        <nav class="plinks" aria-label="More about Sova">
          {sitePages.map((p, i) => <>{i > 0 && <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>}<a href={p.href}>{p.label}</a></>)}
        </nav>
      </header>

      <section class="abstract" id="abstract" aria-label="Abstract">
        <p><b>Abstract.</b> Contracts that accept ZEC today accept a wrapped
        token instead: a claim on coins that a custodian holds on Zcash, on a
        chain whose contracts cannot see Zcash at all. We propose an EVM chain
        whose every node runs a Zcash node, so that a contract can verify a ZEC
        payment against Zcash itself and the ZEC can stay where it was paid. The
        chain’s gas, SOVA, is issued only by destroying ZEC in a transparent
        Zcash transaction that names an EVM address. Burns are grouped by Zcash
        block, one Sova block per Zcash block, so Zcash’s proof-of-work orders
        Sova’s epochs and fixes what each one mints. Each epoch’s fixed reward
        is shared among that epoch’s burners in
        proportion to the ZEC each destroyed, and the largest burner assembles
        the block. Every node re-derives every mint from its own Zcash node and
        rejects a block that disagrees, and every Sova block commits to the Zcash
        block it settles, so a precompile can answer a contract’s questions about
        the transparent Zcash chain with the same answer on every node. Privacy
        is at the funding edge, where a shielded balance funds a burn or a
        payment; execution is public.</p>
      </section>

      <details class="toc-m">
        <summary>Contents</summary>
        <ol class="toc-l">
          {toc.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}><span class="tn">{s.n}</span>{s.t}</a></li>)}
        </ol>
      </details>

      <span id="s1"></span>
      <h2 id="intro">1. Introduction</h2>
      <p>Shielded ZEC is digital cash: it moves without a ledger trail and
      without permission [1]. It cannot run programs. To let a program react to
      a ZEC payment, the practice has been to move the value onto a chain that
      runs programs: a custodian, a multisignature group or a bridge takes the
      ZEC and issues a token in its place. The token is a claim on the
      custodian, and the contracts that use it see the token, never the coin.
      They cannot tell whether a payment happened on Zcash, and the holder has
      given up what made the cash worth programming: possession of it. The
      arrangement works as long as the custodian is honest and solvent, and
      everything built on it inherits that condition.</p>
      <p>What is needed is a chain whose contracts can verify a Zcash payment
      directly, so that the payment can be made on Zcash, to the payee, in the
      payee’s own keys; and whose own asset comes into existence without anyone
      holding anything on the other side.</p>
      <span class="folio" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
    </article>

    <!-- Sheet 2 -->
    <article class="sheet" aria-label="Page 2">
      <p class="runhead" aria-hidden="true"><i>{TITLE}</i><span>pre-release draft</span></p>

      <p>In this paper, we propose an EVM chain that runs beside Zcash and reads
      it. Every Sova node runs its own Zcash node. The chain’s asset, SOVA, is
      issued only when ZEC is destroyed in a Zcash transaction that names the
      address to credit: the burn is the mining act, and afterward the ZEC
      exists for no one. Burns are grouped by Zcash block, one Sova block per
      Zcash block, so Sova’s order is Zcash’s, and every Sova block commits to
      the Zcash block it settles. Because every node derives every mint from
      Zcash, a node can also answer a contract’s question about Zcash
      deterministically, and that is what lets a contract verify a payment.
      Sova changes nothing in Zcash and posts nothing to it.</p>

      <span id="s2"></span>
      <h2 id="burns">2. Burns</h2>
      <p>We define a burn as one transparent Zcash transaction [1, 2] that
      destroys ZEC and names the EVM address to credit. It pays at least 1,000
      zatoshis to the eater script <code>76a914&nbsp;00…00&nbsp;88ac</code>, a
      standard pay-to-public-key-hash output whose key hash is twenty zero bytes.
      No preimage of that hash is known, so the value is unspendable. The
      transaction carries exactly one OP_RETURN output of 29 bytes: the
      two-byte magic “SV”, a version byte, the 20-byte EVM address, and 32
      signal bits for upgrade signaling. The burn’s weight is the ZEC it
      destroys, summed over its eater outputs (Figure 1). The Zcash fee is paid
      to Zcash miners as usual and is not weight.</p>

